(a) The county commissioners may deny a county license under this chapter on any of the following grounds:
(1) Failure to meet state requirements for state license;
(2) Failure to pay required fees and taxes;
(3) Failure to provide required valid information, documents and the like;
(4) False information in the application or attached documents;
(5) Failure to pass review by the sheriff;
(6) Improper residency;
(7) Prior convictions as herein provided;
(8) Failure to meet any other requirements in this chapter for a license of the class applied for;
(9) The proposed premises is inappropriate or unsuitable by reason of disturbances or acts of disorderly conduct, violence or gambling, having occurred in or about said premises;
(10) The existence of dangerous traffic conditions at the proposed premises or a determination by the county commissioners that the granting of a license would cause dangerous traffic conditions as determined by reports from the sheriff's department, department of public safety and/or department of transportation; or
(11) A determination by the county commissioners that the granting of the license would adversely affect the neighborhood or cause a substantial diminution in value of the immediately adjacent and surrounding property.
(b) Otherwise, the county commissioners may issue any county license provided for in this chapter.
(Ord. of 12-6-1988, § 3.1)

